Here's an example PowerShell script to create a symlink to a folder:

```
$target = "C:\my\folder\path"  # Replace with the path of the folder you want to create a symlink to
$link = "C:\my\symlink\path"  # Replace with the path where you want to create the symlink

if (!(test-path $link)) {
    cmd /c mklink /d $link $target
    write-host "Symlink created successfully."
} else {
    write-host "Symlink already exists."
}
```

This script first checks if the symlink already exists, and if it doesn't, it uses the `mklink` command to create a symlink to the specified folder. The `/d` switch specifies that the symlink should be a directory symlink. The script then outputs a message indicating whether the symlink was created successfully or if it already existed.

